// Data of the theorem graph (theorems.html): theorems, lemmas, corollaries and conjectures of theoretical computer
// science, MSC 2020 68 (68P, 68Q, 68R, 68W). In English. Built on 3 November 2026.
//
// Model as in ../../topology/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are the standard
// textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks a proof that
// rests on theory outside these graphs (named in `proof`). `area` is the five-character MSC class, as in
// data/concepts.js. `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// Complexity classes are defined with multitape Turing machines; a statement such as "P ≠ EXP" is a theorem about these
// definitions. Results that hold only under a conjecture (Unique Games, ETH) are not entered as theorems, because a
// conjecture is never a premise.
// References to theorems of other folders: "co:" (pigeonhole principle, Chernoff bound, Fourier analysis on finite
// abelian groups, explicit expanders, Berge's lemma, Euler–Hierholzer), "ca:" (division with remainder for
// polynomials), "ana:" (geometric series), "ent:" (Chinese remainder theorem, primitive roots, best approximation);
// planned: "lo:" (undecidability of the halting problem).
//
// Sources: Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation (3rd ed. 2012); Hopcroft, Motwani, Ullman, Introduction to
// Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation (3rd ed. 2006); Arora and Barak, Computational Complexity: A Modern
// Approach (2009); Goldreich, Foundations of Cryptography I (2001); Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein, Introduction to
// Algorithms (3rd ed. 2009); Li and Vitányi, An Introduction to Kolmogorov Complexity (3rd ed. 2008); Vazirani,
// Approximation Algorithms (2001); Nielsen and Chuang, Quantum Computation and Quantum Information (2000). Not checked
// by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory.

